{"t":1793056202,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"af9d78d4-4745-46f4-a4ca-2cda672d7f72","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-26T18:47:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 261847\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n247 PM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nIncreased cloud cover a bit this evening as some guidance\nindicates a period of standing wave cirrus along the Allegheny\nFront. The next rain potential early next week remains\nuncertain. Otherwise little has changed to the forecast this\nweek.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Dry through the end of October.\n \n- 2) Precipitation chances return in early November.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry through the end of October.\n\nLarge scale ridging persists aloft through the end of the week.\nThis results in dry weather and above normal temperatures \nthrough the end of October. These mild conditions will prevent \nany further frost/freeze threats through the end of the week.\n\nAt the surface, high pressure settles over the Tennessee Valley\ntonight while a weakening front approaches from the north, tied\nto low pressure over the Canadian Maritimes. Without much \nforcing or moisture, no precipitation is expected, only an \nincrease in high clouds and a wind shift to the north and \nnortheast. The front will stall and further weaken over the \narea Tuesday, so there won't be much of a drop in temperatures\ncompared to today. Wednesday may be a few degrees cooler with \nextra cloud cover from a combination of warm advection aloft and\nonshore flow at the surface.\n\nStrong surface high pressure will build southward along the Mid\nAtlantic coast in the wake of the weak front. As winds turn\nsoutherly Thursday and skies clear, temperatures will rise\nnotably above normal. That trend will likely continue Friday \nwhen highs in the mid to upper 70s are possible.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Precipitation chances return in early November.\n\nUpper-level ridging overhead breaks down into zonal flow beginning \nFriday and into Saturday. Conditions likely start to become cloudier \nthroughout the day Saturday as troughing develops over the Ohio \nRiver Valley before heading east Sunday into Monday. This \nlikely brings the first chances for precipitation over the Mid- \nAtlantic in over a week, although overall breadth and total \nrainfall from this system remain uncertain at this time. \n\nTemperatures likely drop back to the low-70s over the weekend \nin the wake of another weak front. With overnight lows \ngenerally remaining in the 40s and 50s, this ranges somewhat \nabove normal for the beginning of November. Looking ahead, model\nguidance hints at a second, potentially more potent upper-level\ntrough approaching the area that could bring additional chances\nfor precipitation sometime next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected to prevail through Tuesday. Wind\ndirection has been highly variable today but should settle on a\nwest-northwest direction before shifting northerly late tonight\nas a dry front pushes through. Fog looks to be less likely\ntonight due to milder temperatures and some mixing along the\nfront.\n\nWinds will become onshore Tuesday night through Wednesday night.\nWhile there isn't a clear signal yet, some low clouds or fog are\npossible, especially at CHO and MRB. Light southerly winds and\nVFR conditions are expected Thursday as high pressure builds\nalong the coast.\n\nPrevailing VFR conditions are anticipated Friday through \nSaturday. The next chance for rain showers occurs Sunday with an\nincoming cold front, but uncertainty remains high at this time.\nWesterly winds Friday shift easterly Saturday. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nLight and highly variable winds have been observed through much\nof today but should take on a northwesterly direction by\nevening. Winds will shift to the north and northeast behind a \ndry cold front late tonight into Tuesday morning. A period of\nelevated winds could potentially channel down the bay early \nTuesday morning. Some models show increasing winds as early as \nmidnight, but most have a very brief period of 20 kt gust\npotential (2-3 hours perhaps) around sunrise. Therefore \nconfidence is not high enough to issue an advisory at this time,\nas the latter scenario could be handled with a Marine Weather \nStatement.\n\nWinds become easterly Tuesday night into Wednesday before\nturning southerly Thursday. Some channeling is possible at \ntimes, though overall gradients look weak. Winds stay below SCA\nthresholds through the end of the month. Southerly winds shift \nwesterly on Friday, then northeasterly on Saturday.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTide levels remain a bit elevated, but anomalies continue to\nslowly decrease overall. Further minor flooding is not expected\nthrough Tuesday night. Onshore flow Wednesday may push \nsensitive shoreline like Annapolis closer to minor flood stage.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/SRT\nAVIATION...ADS/SRT\nMARINE...ADS/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1793056166,"days":{"2026-10-26":{"hi":null,"lo":50,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Clear"},"2026-10-27":{"hi":69,"lo":49,"pop":2,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-28":{"hi":69,"lo":51,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-29":{"hi":72,"lo":52,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-30":{"hi":77,"lo":53,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-31":{"hi":72,"lo":53,"pop":2,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-01":{"hi":70,"lo":56,"pop":18,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-11-02":{"hi":71,"lo":null,"pop":18,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1793055600,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,nu